course: dictionary
lesson: 11442
title: Adequately: well enough
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Adequately is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done. It means well enough, enough for what is needed, but not especially well. «The tent was adequately strong to keep us dry in the wind.» The tent did its job. It was not the best tent in the world, just strong enough.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. If something is adequate, it is enough for the need. «Please make sure the classroom is adequately heated.» The room must be warm enough to be comfortable and safe, not roasting hot.
explain: Adequately is not the same as excellently. «My report was adequately written» means it was good enough, not amazing. Ask yourself: is this enough for what was needed? If yes, adequately is the word to use.
fact: Adequately is an adverb: it tells you how something is done.
fact: «Adequately» means well enough, or in a way that is sufficient for the job.
fact: It does not mean excellent or perfect; it means enough.
fact: «Not adequately» means not enough, so something needs more care or more of something.
fact: Useful phrases: «adequately prepared», «adequately explained», «adequately supervised».
exercise: «The bike lights were adequately bright for the dark lane.» What does adequately mean here? | *Well enough for the job | Extremely bright, brighter than the sun | Flashing very quickly | Not bright at all
exercise: «Our teacher said the rules were not adequately explained.» What does not adequately mean? | Explained twice | *Not enough, so more explanation was needed | Explained by a famous writer | Explained in a very clear and perfect way
exercise: «She had prepared adequately for the test.» Find and copy the adverb that tells how she prepared. | prepared | *adequately | test | she
test: «The fence is not adequately high to keep the dog in.» What is the problem with the fence? | It is perfectly high | It is painted green | It is too tall and heavy | *It is too low to do its job
test: «Mum said my jacket was adequately warm, but not very cosy.» What does this suggest? | It had no warmth at all | It was boiling hot and too thick | It was the warmest jacket in the shop | *It was warm enough for the cold day
exam: Ella says «adequately» means perfectly. Is she right? | Yes, it means perfect and excellent | Yes, it means the same as very bad | No, it means not at all | *No, it means well enough, not perfectly
